1
00:00:23,977 --> 00:00:30,764
Hello and welcome to the NPTEL MOOCs course on Foundations for Virtual and Augmented Reality Systems.

2
00:00:30,784 --> 00:00:37,211
We are going to start a new lecture, Lecture Number 11, on AR or Augmented Reality Software.

3
00:00:39,113 --> 00:00:42,396
So, just a quick recap.

4
00:00:42,737 --> 00:00:47,241
So, in the previous lecture, we talked about virtual reality software.

5
00:00:47,261 --> 00:00:53,528
There we talked about various components that are desirable in a virtual reality software.

6
00:00:54,875 --> 00:01:07,248
with respect to one abstraction that we called virtual world generator, and also we have gone through some popular tools and packages and software,

7
00:01:07,248 --> 00:01:16,874
that are available for different hardware platforms or for building and deploying systems on the web.

8
00:01:17,914 --> 00:01:24,460
So, in this lecture, we are going to do the same for AR or augmented reality, the other technology area that

9
00:01:25,402 --> 00:01:27,625
that we are discussing.

10
00:01:31,710 --> 00:01:38,818
Like VR, in the case of augmented reality also, there are numerous software tools for implementation.

11
00:01:39,579 --> 00:01:45,126
So, a developer can access a large number of such tools.

12
00:01:45,807 --> 00:01:53,316
Now, the thing is, for a better understanding, a structured view of those tools will be beneficial

13
00:01:54,157 --> 00:02:06,089
So, what we will do is we will try to arrange all the tools that are available in the form of a hierarchy depending on decreasing programming skills required.

14
00:02:06,790 --> 00:02:17,240
So, some tools may require a very high level of skill, whereas others may require no skills at all; accordingly, we will try to organize them in some categories.

15
00:02:20,163 --> 00:02:22,105
So, at one extreme of the category

16
00:02:24,312 --> 00:02:33,081
are the low-level software libraries that require considerable skill on the part of the developer.

17
00:02:33,341 --> 00:02:40,869
Skill in terms of understanding the library functions as well as skills in terms of coding where the library functions can be called.

18
00:02:44,833 --> 00:02:48,957
So, that is one extreme where low-level libraries are used.

19
00:02:48,977 --> 00:02:54,302
At the other extreme, we have simple authoring tools which

20
00:02:55,008 --> 00:03:00,977
assume that the developer does not have any programming skill.

21
00:03:00,997 --> 00:03:18,524
So, the tools are typically for novice developers or novice users; users themselves can be developers which assumes that no programming skill is required.

22
00:03:18,544 --> 00:03:23,412
Now, if we categorize it in this way, then we can come up with

23
00:03:23,965 --> 00:03:38,343
four categories: low-level software libraries or frameworks, rapid prototyping tools, plugins for existing developer tools, and finally, standalone AR authoring tools.

24
00:03:38,363 --> 00:03:46,733
So, this is one extreme where no coding skill may be required, whereas this other one, low-level libraries, is another extreme where

25
00:03:47,101 --> 00:03:53,851
a significant amount of coding skills and programming skills from the developer's side will be required.

26
00:03:55,273 --> 00:04:01,823
So, skill-wise, the low-level software libraries or frameworks require strong programming or coding ability.

27
00:04:03,185 --> 00:04:13,180
Some examples of such libraries or frameworks are AR Toolkit, OSG Art, StudiersTube, MXR Toolkit, and so on.

28
00:04:13,821 --> 00:04:16,365
Some of these are no longer available, but those are

29
00:04:17,999 --> 00:04:18,380
there.

30
00:04:21,785 --> 00:04:26,809
In the second category, rapid prototyping tools, the skills required are some programming ability,

31
00:04:26,809 --> 00:04:35,927
lower level as compared to the previous one, but design prototyping skills may be required for using such tools.

32
00:04:36,689 --> 00:04:43,559
Examples of such tools are FLAR manager, OpenFrameworks, and so on.

33
00:04:45,342 --> 00:04:45,863
Then we have

34
00:04:46,417 --> 00:04:48,820
plugins for existing developer tools.

35
00:04:49,481 --> 00:04:58,472
Here the skill required will be the skill with the original tool with which the plugin is being used.

36
00:04:58,832 --> 00:05:05,100
So, the developer needs to know the original tool where the plugin is being used.

37
00:05:06,462 --> 00:05:09,806
And finally, standalone AR authoring tools.

38
00:05:09,826 --> 00:05:12,930
These do not assume any programming ability

39
00:05:15,053 --> 00:05:19,860
So, without any knowledge, such tools can be used.

40
00:05:19,880 --> 00:05:29,072
So, examples for plugins are AR media plugins, Vuforia, MetaIO Unity plugins, Dart, and so on.

41
00:05:29,413 --> 00:05:35,201
Some of these were very popular earlier, but are no longer being supported.

42
00:05:36,122 --> 00:05:39,627
Some are quite popular still today, such as Vuforia.

43
00:05:42,205 --> 00:05:48,691
Standalone AR tools examples are BuildAR, MetaIO Creator, Wikitude, and so on.

44
00:05:50,072 --> 00:05:57,599
Again, some of these are no longer supported.

45
00:05:57,619 --> 00:06:01,683
So, one way of categorization is based on the skill.

46
00:06:02,564 --> 00:06:04,886
There can be other types of categorizations also.

47
00:06:04,906 --> 00:06:10,491
Sometimes we can categorize based on target application platforms.

48
00:06:11,078 --> 00:06:18,785
So, some tools are meant for desktop AR applications while others are meant for mobile AR applications.

49
00:06:20,547 --> 00:06:23,530
Again, categorization can be based on the technology.

50
00:06:24,951 --> 00:06:34,841
So, some support CV-based tracking, computer vision-based tracking, whereas others use other sensors for tracking such as GPS sensors.

51
00:06:35,261 --> 00:06:37,103
So, such technology-based

52
00:06:37,505 --> 00:06:45,694
categorization or platform-based categorization or skill-based categorization is possible with AR tools.

53
00:06:48,077 --> 00:06:54,465
But we should try to understand the tools in terms of some sort of categorization for a structured view.

54
00:06:57,368 --> 00:07:01,693
So let us assume the skill-based categorization and accordingly let us discuss the tools.

55
00:07:02,254 --> 00:07:04,356
So we'll start with the

56
00:07:05,180 --> 00:07:14,149
the one which requires the maximum skill from the developer, that is the low-level libraries and frameworks.

57
00:07:14,169 --> 00:07:14,990
So, what is the idea?

58
00:07:17,172 --> 00:07:24,759
Low-level libraries provide access to the core tracking and display functionality to build an AR application.

59
00:07:25,460 --> 00:07:27,442
A popular example is AR Toolkit.

60
00:07:27,462 --> 00:07:28,943
There are many other such examples.

61
00:07:30,645 --> 00:07:33,027
Now, if we talk about AR Toolkit.

62
00:07:34,205 --> 00:07:40,635
It is one of the most widely adopted software libraries for developing AR applications.

63
00:07:40,655 --> 00:07:51,644
It can provide marker-based tracking in its open-source version, or there is a commercial version available for AR toolkit which can be accessed from AR tool works,

64
00:07:51,644 --> 00:08:01,005
which also supports tracking natural features on an arbitrary image, that is, it uses computer vision techniques.

65
00:08:04,327 --> 00:08:12,379
The toolkit is written in C, and it supports various platforms such as Windows, Linux, and macOS.

66
00:08:18,748 --> 00:08:24,978
One thing to note here is that the toolkit, ARToolKit, is primarily meant to provide tracking support.

67
00:08:25,098 --> 00:08:28,022
Tracking we have already discussed in one of the previous lectures.

68
00:08:29,116 --> 00:08:37,188
It tracks various things including objects in the environment, and AR and VR systems are supposed to have tracking functions inbuilt.

69
00:08:38,530 --> 00:08:54,837
Now, if we are using ARToolKit to develop an AR application, then the toolkit is a library which provides functions that can enable the developer to perform tracking and accordingly do some activity.

70
00:08:56,453 --> 00:09:04,562
The primary purpose of ARToolKit is to provide tracking support to the developer.

71
00:09:04,582 --> 00:09:13,272
As a consequence, the toolkit provides only low-level OpenGL-based rendering support.

72
00:09:13,773 --> 00:09:22,663
So, very primitive rendering support is provided and no built-in support for interaction techniques or complex realistic graphics.

73
00:09:22,930 --> 00:09:26,429
So, it can be used to render, but at a very low level,

74
00:09:26,429 --> 00:09:35,522
very simple level rather than realistic graphics, and also it does not provide support for interaction with the digital content overlaid on the real-world view.

75
00:09:38,305 --> 00:09:52,819
So, if we are going to use it alone, it may still result in some system, but that system will be very rudimentary, and to provide interaction the developer has to write

76
00:09:53,221 --> 00:09:55,604
things from scratch, develop things from scratch.

77
00:09:56,005 --> 00:10:02,072
So, it will be difficult and time-consuming to develop a complete AR application which looks realistic.

78
00:10:05,717 --> 00:10:09,602
Now, that is the problem if we are going to use the ARToolKit alone.

79
00:10:11,244 --> 00:10:13,727
So, to overcome this problem, what we can do is.

80
00:10:15,057 --> 00:10:21,286
Now, this problem typically happens with many standard toolkits or low-level libraries.

81
00:10:21,526 --> 00:10:30,608
They primarily provide tracking support and miss certain desired features that are required for realistic rendering of digital content.

82
00:10:30,608 --> 00:10:40,272
on real-world view that is typically required in an AR application, and also provide interaction support.

83
00:10:42,243 --> 00:10:51,418
So to overcome that, what we can do is we can combine multiple libraries and create a framework for AR system development.

84
00:10:51,438 --> 00:10:58,008
So we can call the combination of those libraries a framework for the developers.

85
00:11:01,955 --> 00:11:07,744
Let us see an example with AR toolkit, what kind of framework we can use or create.

86
00:11:08,416 --> 00:11:12,920
So, we can overcome the limitations of AR toolkit by combining other libraries.

87
00:11:14,221 --> 00:11:21,628
Typically, we should look for libraries that provide interaction as well as realistic graphic support.

88
00:11:21,648 --> 00:11:32,999
One such type of library can be libraries with support for scene graph management.

89
00:11:33,019 --> 00:11:36,162
Now, why are we emphasizing the term scene graphs?

90
00:11:36,614 --> 00:11:42,041
Scene graphs represent computer graphic scenes as a node-based graph.

91
00:11:42,061 --> 00:11:52,299
So, essentially the entire scene is represented in the form of a tree with nodes and links between nodes, and we can selectively work with

92
00:11:52,299 --> 00:12:00,124
nodes to manipulate the scene and also create realistic effects and also allow interaction.

93
00:12:03,108 --> 00:12:14,524
So it is actually a very nice way of trying to represent the complexity of a scene as well as trying to work with the complexity of a scene.

94
00:12:15,285 --> 00:12:19,831
So it provides a way to manage the functionality of a complex graphics application.

95
00:12:20,272 --> 00:12:28,142
So scene graph-based representations generally aid in graphics applications.

96
00:12:28,162 --> 00:12:32,929
Some of the features that typically scene graph-based representations provide

97
00:12:33,837 --> 00:12:34,518
have.

98
00:12:35,499 --> 00:12:43,810
So, they allow sorting objects according to visibility: which are visible with respect to viewer position and which should not be visible.

99
00:12:46,213 --> 00:12:50,858
It can allow removing objects out of camera field of view.

100
00:12:50,878 --> 00:12:56,866
So, this one is called visible surface detection, and this one is called clipping.

101
00:12:57,327 --> 00:13:00,030
So, these operations that are typically part of any

102
00:13:00,280 --> 00:13:06,990
3D graphics pipeline are easily managed and supported by this type of representation.

103
00:13:08,412 --> 00:13:13,399
Also, it can allow state management and higher level abstractions of functionalities.

104
00:13:14,741 --> 00:13:19,187
So, many things are possible if we go with scene graph-based representation.

105
00:13:21,050 --> 00:13:24,415
So, in a sense, if we summarize

106
00:13:25,930 --> 00:13:41,187
If we take help of a scene graph library, then that can enable the developer to focus on interactions between virtual objects rather than low-level object rendering.

107
00:13:42,949 --> 00:13:49,036
So, earlier with only AR Toolkit, the focus was on tracking.

108
00:13:49,417 --> 00:13:54,823
So, low-level object rendering plus interaction both had to be taken care of by the developer.

109
00:13:55,900 --> 00:14:02,021
Now, if we combine the AR toolkit with scene graph libraries,

110
00:14:02,021 --> 00:14:14,339
Any of the scene graph libraries, then these low level rendering challenges can be taken care of by the library and the developer can focus more on the interaction part.

111
00:14:17,782 --> 00:14:24,709
So, one such open source library, one such scene graph library is

112
00:14:25,297 --> 00:14:29,363
The Open Scene Graph, it used to be quite popular earlier.

113
00:14:29,443 --> 00:14:33,669
Now, there is a successor of OSG that is Vulkan Scene Graph VSG.

114
00:14:34,971 --> 00:14:38,996
Both are open source and widely used.

115
00:14:39,016 --> 00:14:40,538
There are other such libraries as well.

116
00:14:41,380 --> 00:14:43,422
We are taking these as examples.

117
00:14:45,886 --> 00:14:50,292
Now, it is written in C++ language.

118
00:14:53,107 --> 00:15:07,560
And such libraries are built on top of another library that is called OpenGL, which accesses the lower-level hardware.

119
00:15:07,580 --> 00:15:18,700
The advantage is that with this library is that it supports a large number of plugins for additional functionality such as model loading, shadow rendering,

120
00:15:18,700 --> 00:15:23,113
physics which are primarily relevant most of the time for VR and

121
00:15:23,819 --> 00:15:26,782
Partly relevant for AR.

122
00:15:33,588 --> 00:15:41,996
Through this example, we can try to understand the concept of a framework based on AR toolkit and OSG.

123
00:15:42,016 --> 00:15:51,244
So, it is, suppose we have this marker on a table; when we point our mobile phone,

124
00:15:51,562 --> 00:16:00,054
the phone detects the marker and then a 3D object, that is a cow, pops up on your screen.

125
00:16:02,538 --> 00:16:14,375
So, then it involves two parts: tracking, that is recognizing the marker and its position, and then 3D registration, that is creating and rendering the cow on that position.

126
00:16:15,337 --> 00:16:17,620
So, these operations can be

127
00:16:18,207 --> 00:16:24,636
taken care of by combining AR toolkit and OSG or VSG, its successor.

128
00:16:25,517 --> 00:16:28,421
The entire thing can be represented in the form of a node graph.

129
00:16:29,983 --> 00:16:46,746
So, we can have a root node, then the virtual camera can be represented as a node and we can work with parameters of the node. Projection that is part of rendering can be considered

130
00:16:47,198 --> 00:16:50,944
as another node which allows projection on the video layer.

131
00:16:52,526 --> 00:16:55,772
Other related concepts can be considered as nodes.

132
00:16:55,792 --> 00:16:59,437
The 3D object itself, the cow, can be considered as a node.

133
00:17:00,018 --> 00:17:08,932
Then various transformations that are part of 3D graphics can be represented as a single node or individual nodes.

134
00:17:11,316 --> 00:17:13,780
So, once we are able to represent this entire

135
00:17:16,645 --> 00:17:26,962
activity in the form of a graph, then it is easier to visualize and manipulate such a graph to create the desired effect that the user is looking for.

136
00:17:29,245 --> 00:17:35,736
So, here the simple scene graph contains video input and tracking.

137
00:17:37,178 --> 00:17:42,687
Both video input primarily comes from the

138
00:17:44,118 --> 00:17:56,236
input device. Tracking again based on sensors present in the input device, in this case camera, combined with nodes for graphics rendering.

139
00:17:57,437 --> 00:18:06,090
So, various nodes like transformation, 3D object representation, orthographic projection; these are related to graphics rendering.

140
00:18:06,731 --> 00:18:12,960
So, when we combine these, the end result is a simple AR scene with a 3D cow model overlaid

141
00:18:13,565 --> 00:18:15,427
on a tracking marker.

142
00:18:15,888 --> 00:18:24,549
Now, this part overlaid on a tracking marker that is 3D registration and tracking is taken care of by AR Toolkit whereas the other part,

143
00:18:24,549 --> 00:18:32,628
AR scene creation with a 3D cow that is taken care of by the OSG that is Open Scene Graph.

144
00:18:37,454 --> 00:18:42,981
So, there are many such frameworks available, some are still in use, some were

145
00:18:43,720 --> 00:18:54,732
being used earlier quite popular for example OSG ART 3 library it links AR Toolkit for tracking support with OSG library for real-time rendering features

146
00:18:54,732 --> 00:19:05,156
Goblin XNA combines ART tag tracking library so this ART tag is another tracking library with Microsoft XNA game platform

147
00:19:05,156 --> 00:19:12,057
Metaio SDK plus OSG the former provides a comprehensive set of AR tracking technologies

148
00:19:13,488 --> 00:19:17,452
Metaio SDK whereas OSG provides rendering support.

149
00:19:18,714 --> 00:19:22,779
So this is one way of looking at the software.

150
00:19:25,442 --> 00:19:28,185
Now there can be mobile-specific frameworks as well.

151
00:19:28,225 --> 00:19:30,347
For example, AR Toolkit for mobile.

152
00:19:31,128 --> 00:19:39,938
It is a version of the AR Toolkit available for mobile platforms including iOS and Android, integrated with OpenSceneGraph but developed for mobile.

153
00:19:40,579 --> 00:19:41,740
Then we have DroidAR.

154
00:19:42,479 --> 00:19:44,082
And also we have Vuforia.

155
00:19:44,562 --> 00:19:46,585
Vuforia is still quite popularly used.

156
00:19:50,952 --> 00:19:58,324
Now, various libraries, as we have mentioned earlier, support tracking, but they can support it in different ways.

157
00:19:58,945 --> 00:20:08,520
For example, computer vision can be used to support tracking, which is there in Vuforia, or other sensors can be used for tracking support.

158
00:20:09,209 --> 00:20:14,915
For example, DroidAR uses other sensors for tracking support.

159
00:20:19,160 --> 00:20:21,923
That is about low-level libraries and frameworks.

160
00:20:21,943 --> 00:20:31,234
Next, let us shift our discussion to the second category: rapid prototyping and development tools.

161
00:20:36,766 --> 00:20:42,854
Now, these low-level libraries and frameworks provide maximum flexibility in development.

162
00:20:44,456 --> 00:20:53,128
We all know that if you have a skill and you understand the libraries, you can combine them in any way you want and can come up with your own design.

163
00:20:54,930 --> 00:21:00,377
But it can be time consuming and requires significant programming skill.

164
00:21:01,792 --> 00:21:10,706
To implement an AR application using the library, first you have to know the libraries or the framework and then write programs to implement the tool.

165
00:21:10,726 --> 00:21:12,449
So that requires time and skill.

166
00:21:13,771 --> 00:21:21,442
Sometimes it is helpful to rapidly prototype or visualize an idea to show to the end users or clients.

167
00:21:22,644 --> 00:21:25,048
We all know the importance of prototypes.

168
00:21:27,560 --> 00:21:32,128
Before actually undertaking any major development exercise that involves coding.

169
00:21:33,850 --> 00:21:38,438
So, if some idea is not good, then no further coding will be required.

170
00:21:38,478 --> 00:21:50,698
So, if we can visualize such ideas beforehand using simple means rather than actually going through the entire coding exercise, then that will be beneficial as we all understand.

171
00:21:53,615 --> 00:22:13,178
So one such popular authoring tool for creating prototypes having multimedia content for the web and desktop platform is provided by the popular Adobe Flash tool.

172
00:22:18,144 --> 00:22:21,382
So that tool can be used to prototype

173
00:22:21,382 --> 00:22:36,546
a variety of desktop and mobile experiences by using experiences related to AR by using AR plugins that are available such as FLAR Toolkit or FLAR Manager.

174
00:22:43,354 --> 00:22:47,398
Now, let us look at the FLAR Toolkit.

175
00:22:47,969 --> 00:22:51,272
It is a version of AR toolkit ported over to Flash.

176
00:22:52,373 --> 00:22:56,738
It allows people to have AR experience from within their web browser.

177
00:23:02,063 --> 00:23:16,677
If a user is using the toolkit, the FLAR toolkit, to prototype an AR system or AR idea, then with a few lines of code, the user can

178
00:23:17,264 --> 00:23:23,470
activate their camera to get the experience, of course.

179
00:23:23,490 --> 00:23:29,617
look for AR markers in the camera view; tracking that is part of tracking.

180
00:23:30,838 --> 00:23:36,564
load and overlay virtual content onto the tracked image for 3D registering.

181
00:23:38,786 --> 00:23:44,672
So, all three activities, or the two primary activities like tracking and 3D

182
00:23:45,040 --> 00:23:50,627
can be implemented using the FLAR Toolkit or FLAR toolkit.

183
00:23:54,792 --> 00:23:58,805
In that way, since all of these can be done with a few lines of code,

184
00:23:58,805 --> 00:24:10,052
then it makes it possible for anyone with a FLASH-enabled web browser and camera connected to a computer to have or develop an AR experience with minimal effort.

185
00:24:13,593 --> 00:24:19,039
Similarly, there is a framework called the Flash AR Manager or FLAR Manager.

186
00:24:19,739 --> 00:24:22,022
It is the equivalent of a framework we discussed earlier.

187
00:24:22,582 --> 00:24:32,452
It combines a variety of different AR tracking and graphics libraries together, but primarily for prototyping purposes.

188
00:24:32,472 --> 00:24:35,635
So, combinations can be in different ways.

189
00:24:36,376 --> 00:24:42,182
So, if on the one hand we have an AR tracking library, we can use the FLAR Toolkit.

190
00:24:43,428 --> 00:24:45,270
FLAR Tracker, NFT.

191
00:24:45,850 --> 00:24:56,659
And on the other hand, if we are combining it with a 3D graphics library, then we can use alternative 3D, Away 3D, Away 3D Lite, Paper Vision 3D, Sandy 3D.

192
00:24:56,679 --> 00:25:03,345
So, these are some examples of different graphics libraries that can be combined with the toolkits.

193
00:25:04,386 --> 00:25:12,473
But both in this case can be used; a combination of both can be used.

194
00:25:12,773 --> 00:25:21,364
For primarily for prototyping rather than going for a full-fledged development exercise.

195
00:25:21,384 --> 00:25:32,559
Although we have discussed such prototyping tools using Flash only, you can understand that Flash-based tools are not the only ones available.

196
00:25:32,579 --> 00:25:42,392
Non-Flash-based authoring tools are also there, but they may not be as popular as the Flash-based tools because of the

197
00:25:42,963 --> 00:25:44,146
penetration in the market.

198
00:25:46,211 --> 00:25:50,581
Now, such other tools we are going to discuss in the next part of the lecture.

199
00:25:53,588 --> 00:25:56,415
So, just to summarize whatever we have covered up to this point.

200
00:25:57,678 --> 00:26:04,126
We have got the broader idea of AR software, particularly in terms of a structured representation using categories.

201
00:26:04,146 --> 00:26:14,619
So, different ways to categorize them we have seen: either skill-based categorization, skill requirement-based categorization, or platform-based categorization, or technology-based categorization.

202
00:26:16,041 --> 00:26:27,195
We are following skill requirement-based categorization, and based on that we have started our discussion with the low-level libraries and frameworks, then we moved to

203
00:26:27,901 --> 00:26:31,667
authoring tools for building prototypes, which we are continuing.

204
00:26:32,829 --> 00:26:34,592
So, we have covered that category partly.

